# What this package may never name, anywhere under lib/ and test/.
#
# THE TEST FOR A WORD: could a vendor with the same tool and a completely different product still
# use this package with that word in it? A name the tool gives itself passes and stays off this
# list. A name of one product built on a Linux host fails and goes on it.
#
# microk8s is NOT on this list, and that is a decision rather than an oversight: the MicroK8s snap
# is one of the tools this package declares and drives — its manifest names it beside apt, dpkg,
# snap, sshd, systemd and netplan — and the word is the name that snap gives itself. On the lists
# of the other tool packages the same word IS forbidden, because there it names a product's choice
# of substrate rather than the tool in front of the step.
#
# One word per line. Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored; everything else is matched
# case-insensitively and word-anchored, so a longer name that merely contains a word is no
# occurrence of it.
#
# THE LIST LIVES HERE, UNDER tool/, AND NOT BESIDE THE CHECK THAT READS IT. The scan reads test/ to
# the byte, so a list written into test/checks/tool_purity_test.dart would itself be an occurrence
# of every word on it and the check could never pass. That tool/ stays out of the scan is asserted
# by a counter-probe of its own.

# The product this package was cut out of, and the organisation behind it.
hostyour
digita
simetrix

# That product's choices: the components it deploys, the vendors it rents from, and the name it
# gave its certificate issuer. Each is a fact of one product, not of any tool this package drives.
argocd
tekton
zot
authentik
cloudflare
hetzner
letsencrypt-prod

# That product's own domain words.
tenant
consumer
